-- title:
--   Theorem 3.4 — secret recovery cost $\mathrm{poly}(\log p)[1+\min\{|\mathcal B_1|,(KZ_{\mathrm{target}})^k\}]$ for every application
-- statement:
--   This is the generic cost theorem of the recovery framework.
--
--   Fix $k,m\ge1$, $r\ge1$ and constants $C_0,K_0$. There are constants $C,K$ such that the following holds for every application with $k$ error variables and $m$ polynomials that satisfies requirements (i)–(iv) with bound $C_0(\log p)^{K_0}$, and every reconstructor with constant $r$ and expected cost at most $C_0(\log p)^{K_0}$. There is one randomized machine (Algorithm 1 with this reconstructor) such that, for every public input:
--
--   1. with probability one it returns exactly the set of secrets satisfying the original input constraints;
--   2. its expected bit time is at most
--   $$
--   C(\log p)^K\bigl[1+\min\{|\mathcal B_1|,(KZ_{\mathrm{target}})^k\}\bigr],\qquad K=1+12\sqrt d\,4^m,
--   $$
--   where $d$ is the number of distinct nonconstant monomials of $P$ and $Z_{\mathrm{target}}=\max\{1,(p\lambda_{\mathrm{target}})^{-1}\}$ for the lattice generated by $\Phi(P_1),\dots,\Phi(P_m)$ and $v_{\mathrm{tar}}=\Phi(px_1)$;
--   3. if the reconstructor is deterministic, the machine is deterministic and every run obeys this bound.
--
--   The expectation is over the reconstruction randomness for a fixed public input. Sections 4–6 obtain their cost bounds by averaging this per-input bound over the sampled inputs.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The two occurrences of $K$ are different: the exponent $K$ of $\log p$ is a constant of the theorem, and $K_{m,d}=1+12\sqrt d\,4^m$ (`Kmd`) is the constant of (2). $|\mathcal B_1|$ is the number of integers in the first error interval.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: "Algorithm 1, with R" for an application → for every application satisfying the requirements and every reconstructor machine there is a machine M with the stated behaviour; reason: the theorem is generic over applications; the paper's algorithm is the witness
--   - reading: Algorithm 1, its (expected) bit time, Las Vegas and probability one → one randomized multi-tape Turing machine (MTMachine, alphabet Fin 8, one fair bit per tick); bit time = ticks, expected bit time = lower integral of the halting time under the fair-coin measure, probability one = almost surely; reason: the paper names no machine model; its bounds are linear in the number of tests, so the model may lose only a polylogarithmic factor on the proof's bookkeeping (balanced search trees), which multi-tape machines do and single-tape machines need not
--   - reading: (iv) the full polynomials P_i can be constructed deterministically in poly(log p) bit time → a deterministic machine outputs p, X, the endpoints of the intervals B_i and the coefficient lists of P_1, …, P_m within C₀(log p)^{K₀} ticks; reason: p, X and B belong to the public input and the algorithm reads them; the same applications qualify
--   - reading: p (the modulus of §3) → p prime, X ≥ 1; reason: standing assumption of Definition 2.1 (the public parameters are a prime p); §3.1 states X ≥ 1
--   - encoding: poly(log p) depending on k, m, d, r and the polynomial bounds of the hypotheses → C(log p)^K with C, K depending on k, m, r, C₀, K₀ only; reason: d ≤ k + k(k + 1)/2 for polynomials of degree at most two, so constants for each d up to that bound can be maximized
--   - encoding: returns the set in Proposition 3.3 → almost surely halts with the canonical code of the finite set of secrets y for which some x ∈ B satisfies the constraints; reason: that set is the set of Proposition 3.3; sets are coded by sorted secret codes
-- source:
--   Kang, Lee, Provable Recovery from Integer Quotients: Improved Bounds for Nonlinear Hidden Number Problems, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256 605f9dd3…), Theorem 3.4, p.20 (PDF p.21); §3.1 and Definition 3.1, p.16 (PDF p.17); Proposition 3.3, p.18 (PDF p.19); §3.3 and eq. (2), pp.19–20 (PDF pp.20–21)

